<Base title="Arrays · ITC 120" description="Arrays in plain English: access, traversal, insertion, and deletion, with a step-by-step lab and Java code." course={{ code: course.code, href: base + course.href }} toc>
  <header class="wrap lesson-hero flow">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href={base + course.href}>ITC 120</a> › Prelim › Arrays</p>
    <p class="kicker">Linear data structures · Lesson 1 of 4</p>
    <h1>Arrays: the four operations</h1>
    <KindChips />
    <p class="lede">Access, traversal, insertion, and deletion, and why some of them cost more than others.</p>
    <div class="goals">
      <p class="note-label">By the end, you can:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>describe the four basic operations on an array (access, traversal, insertion, deletion), and write access and traversal in Java;</li>
        <li>show which values move when you insert or delete in the middle, and in which order;</li>
        <li>compare arrays and linked lists, and choose one for a task.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <p class="muted small"><b>You need:</b> the <a href={base + '/itc120/programming-refresher/'}>Programming refresher</a> (especially loops and arrays), the <a href={base + '/itc120/intro/'}>Introduction</a>, and scratch paper.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="four">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 1 · The big idea</p>
      <h2>Four things we do with data</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="mini-arr" aria-label="An array named arr with values 19, 3, 42, 7, 56 at indexes 0 to 4">
        <span class="nm"><b>arr</b><i></i></span>{[19, 3, 42, 7, 56].map((v, i) => <span class={i === 2 ? 'hot' : ''}><b>{v}</b><i>{i}</i></span>)}
      </div>
      <p>This lesson is about the <b>array</b>, the first linear data structure of the course. Here are both words again:</p>
      <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Array and index</p><p>An <b>array</b> is a fixed number of slots, side by side, that all hold the same type of value. Each slot's numbered position is its <b>index</b>, counting from 0.</p></div>
      <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Linear data structure</p><p>A data structure whose items sit one after another, in a line. Each item has one item before it and one after it, except the first and the last. Arrays, linked lists, stacks, and queues are linear.</p></div>
      <p>The array above is named <code>arr</code>. Slot 2 holds 42, so <code>arr[2]</code> is 42. Think of a <b>piso-net shop</b>: a fixed row of numbered units, one user per PC.</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={pisonetPic()} /><figcaption>Unit numbers work like indexes, and each user is like the value in a slot. (Real shops usually number from 1; Java arrays start at 0.)</figcaption></figure>
      <p>Every data structure in this course is judged by how well it does these four operations:</p>
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>Access</b></td><td>Get one value using its index.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Traverse</b></td><td>Visit every value, in order.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Insert</b></td><td>Add a new value at a position.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Delete</b></td><td>Remove the value at a position.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={fourOpsPic()} /><figcaption>The four operations on one small array.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Where you see them</h3>
        <p>Different programs lean on different operations:</p>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Mostly uses</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>The registrar's list of 5,000 students in ID order. Pull up one student, or average them all.</td><td>access and traversal</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A sari-sari store's inventory. New stock arrives and old items are dropped all day.</td><td>insert and delete</td></tr>
            <tr><td>An office print queue. Jobs join at the back and leave from the front.</td><td>insert and delete, at the ends</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={opsWhere()} /><figcaption>Each situation leans on different operations.</figcaption></figure>
        <p>That is why there is no single best structure. The right one depends on what the program does most.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="access">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 2 · Operation 1</p>
      <h2>Access: one step, every time</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="mini-arr" aria-label="marks: 14, 28, 35, 49, 60; index 2 highlighted">
        <span class="nm"><b>marks</b><i></i></span>{[14, 28, 35, 49, 60].map((v, i) => <span class={i === 2 ? 'hot' : ''}><b>{v}</b><i>{i}</i></span>)}
      </div>
      <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(access)} /></pre>
      <p>Java goes <b>straight to slot 2</b>. It does not look at slots 0 and 1 first. The slots sit side by side in memory, so Java can compute where slot 2 is right away. It takes one step whether the array holds 5 values or 5,000. This is the array's best trick.</p>
      <details class="reveal"><summary>Optional: how can Java jump straight to slot 2?</summary><div class="flow">
        <p>Every spot in the computer's memory has a number, called its <b>address</b>. An array's slots sit side by side, and every slot is the same size (an <code>int</code> takes 4 bytes). So one calculation finds any slot:</p>
        <p><code>address of slot i = start + i × slot size</code></p>
        <p>Here, <b>start</b> is the address where slot 0 begins, <b>i</b> is the index, and <b>slot size</b> is how many bytes one value takes. If <code>marks</code> started at address 1000, slot 2 would start at 1000 + 2 × 4 = <b>1008</b>. No other slot is looked at. This is also one reason indexes start at 0: slot 0 is zero slots away from the start. (Java hides the real addresses from your program, but the idea is the same.)</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={addrPic()} /><figcaption>One calculation finds slot 2. No other slot is checked.</figcaption></figure>
      </div></details>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Watch the last index</p>
        <div class="mini-arr sm" aria-label="marks has slots 0 to 4. There is no slot 5."><span class="nm"><b>marks</b><i></i></span>{[14, 28, 35, 49, 60].map((v, i) => <span><b>{v}</b><i>{i}</i></span>)}<span class="ghost"><b>✗</b><i>5?</i></span></div>
        <p>Indexes start at 0, so the last index is <code>length - 1</code>. For <code>marks</code> (length 5), the last index is 4. There is no slot 5, so <code>marks[5]</code> stops the program with an <b>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</b>. An <b>exception</b> is an error that happens while the program is running; if nothing handles it, the program stops. This is why loops use <code>i &lt; marks.length</code>, not <code>i &lt;= marks.length</code>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="traverse">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 3 · Operation 2</p>
      <h2>Traversal: visit every value</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Traversal</p><p>Visiting every item in a data structure once, in order, to do something with it: print it, add it up, or compare it. In an array, a traversal goes from index 0 to the last index, one slot at a time.</p></div>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={traversePic()} /><figcaption>A traversal of marks: five slots, five visits, then the loop stops.</figcaption></figure>
      <p>A loop does the visiting. Start at 0, keep going while <code>i</code> is less than the length, and add 1 each round:</p>
      <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(traverse)} /></pre>
      <p>You already know this loop. <b>Traversal</b> is just its name in DSA. One pass through the array can answer a question, like "what is the highest score?"</p>
      <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(highest)} /></pre>
      <p><code>highest</code> starts with a real score, <code>marks[0]</code> (78), so the loop can begin at index 1. Here is the trace table:</p>
      <div class="scroll-x">
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>i</th><th>marks[i]</th><th>marks[i] &gt; highest?</th><th>highest after</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td colspan="3">before the loop: <code>highest = marks[0]</code></td><td><b>78</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>1</td><td>85</td><td>85 &gt; 78: yes</td><td><b>85</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>2</td><td>91</td><td>91 &gt; 85: yes</td><td><b>91</b></td></tr>
            <tr><td>3</td><td>74</td><td>74 &gt; 91: no</td><td>91</td></tr>
            <tr><td>4</td><td>88</td><td>88 &gt; 91: no</td><td>91</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Output: <b>91</b>.</p>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Why not start <code>highest</code> at 0?</p>
        <p>It works for quiz scores, but not always. If every value were negative, like temperatures in a freezer, the answer would wrongly be 0. Starting with <code>marks[0]</code> always uses a real value.</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={zeroTrapPic()} /><figcaption>With all-negative values, starting at 0 gives a wrong answer.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Counting with a condition</h3>
        <p>Add a counter and an <code>if</code>, and the same loop can count. This one counts the passing scores (75 and up):</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(count)} /></pre>
        <p>The counter changes only when the condition is true: for 88 and 95. So the output is <code>Passed: 2</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="paper flow">
        <p class="note-label">Try it: predict the output</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(stock)} /></pre>
        <p>Write exactly what this prints. Careful: what does it print, <code>i</code> or <code>stock[i]</code>?</p>
        <details class="reveal"><summary>Show the answer</summary><div class="flow">
          <pre class="code"><code>Restock item 1
Restock item 3
Items to restock: 2</code></pre>
          <p>Only 0 (at index 1) and 3 (at index 3) are less than 5. The loop prints the <b>index</b> <code>i</code>, not the stock value.</p>
        </div></details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="shift">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 4 · Operations 3 and 4</p>
      <h2>Insert and delete: shifting</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>An array's slots sit side by side, and its length is fixed when it is created. So to insert or delete in the middle, other values have to <b>move</b>.</p>
      <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Shifting</p><p>Moving a group of values one slot over, all in the same direction: to the right to open a gap, or to the left to close one.</p></div>
      <p>To know where the values end, programs keep two numbers:</p>
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>capacity</b> (<code>arr.length</code>)</td><td>How many slots the array has. It never changes.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>size</b></td><td>How many slots hold real values right now. We keep it in our own variable.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Insert: later values move right</h3>
        <p>To insert 25 at index 2 of 10, 20, 30, 40:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Move 40 from slot 3 to slot 4.</li>
          <li>Move 30 from slot 2 to slot 3.</li>
          <li>Put 25 into slot 2. Size goes from 4 to 5.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Here is the same insert in Java. Step through it line by line:</p>
        <CodeWalk id="cw-arr-ins" code={arrInsertCode} steps={arrInsertSteps()} cap="Walkthrough: insert 25 at index 2" hint="Use Next to follow the loop, or tap a line to jump to it." />
      </div>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Start from the end</p>
        <p>If we moved 30 first, it would land on top of 40, and 40 would be lost. Moving from the end means each value moves into a slot that is already free. That is why the loop counts <b>down</b>: <code>i--</code>.</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={wrongOrderPic()} /><figcaption>The mistake: moving 30 first covers 40 before it can move.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Check for room first</p>
        <p>Insert needs one free slot. If <code>size == arr.length</code>, every slot is in use, and the loop would write to <code>arr[size]</code>, a slot that does not exist: an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException. So check first:</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(full)} /></pre>
        <p>A Java array cannot grow. To fit more values, a program makes a new, bigger array and copies the values over. (Java's <code>ArrayList</code> does this for you.)</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Delete: later values move left</h3>
        <p>To delete index 1 of 10, 20, 25, 30, 40:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Copy 25 into slot 1, on top of the 20.</li>
          <li>Copy 30 into slot 2, then 40 into slot 3.</li>
          <li>Size drops from 5 to 4. Slot 4 still holds an old copy of 40, but we stop counting it.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Here is the delete in Java, with the same array:</p>
        <CodeWalk id="cw-arr-del" code={arrDeleteCode} steps={arrDeleteSteps()} cap="Walkthrough: delete index 1" hint="Use Next to follow the loop, or tap a line to jump to it." />
        <p>This time the loop counts <b>up</b>, starting at the gap. Each value moves into the slot that was just copied from.</p>
      </div>
      <ArrayLab id="lab-array" />
      <p class="small muted">Try: insert 25 at index 2, then delete index 1. Then insert at index 0 and count the moves.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="cost">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 5 · Why this matters</p>
      <h2>Access stays cheap. Shifting does not.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Count the work. Inserting at the front means <b>every</b> value moves one slot:</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={costPic()} /><figcaption>Access is one jump at any size. Inserting at the front moves every value.</figcaption></figure>
      <p>Here are the counts:</p>
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Work</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Access any index</td><td><b>1 step</b>, no matter the size</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Insert at the front of an array holding 4 values</td><td><b>4 moves</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Insert at the front of an array holding 500 values</td><td><b>500 moves</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Do that 1,000 times</td><td>at least 500 × 1,000 = <b>half a million</b> moves (more, since the array keeps growing)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>So the question is not "is the array good?" It is "good for <b>which</b> operation?" Users never complain about 500 moves. They complain that the app froze.</p>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>A first look ahead: linked lists</h3>
        <p>A <b>linked list</b> stores values in <b>nodes</b>: small boxes that each hold one value and an arrow to the next node. You will learn it next. To insert B2 between B and C, it changes two arrows. A, B, and C never move.</p>
        <div class="mini-ll" aria-label="A to B to B2 to C"><b>A</b>→<b>B</b>→<b style="background:var(--orange);color:#fff;border-color:var(--orange-ink)">B2</b>→<b>C</b>→<i>null</i></div>
        <p>Here is the same operation in both structures:</p>
        <div class="scroll-x">
          <table class="tbl">
            <thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Array</th><th>Linked list</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Get the value at index 500</td><td>one step</td><td>start at the first node and follow 500 links</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Insert, once you are at the spot</td><td>shift every later value</td><td>change two links</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Delete, once you are at the spot</td><td>shift every later value</td><td>change one link (in the node before it)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Memory per item</td><td>just the value</td><td>the value plus a link</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Size</td><td>fixed when created</td><td>grows as you add</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div class="remember"><p class="note-label">Remember</p><p>The linked list only wins once you have already found the spot. Getting there still takes walking.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="practice">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 6 · Your turn</p>
      <h2>Practice</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="paper flow">
        <p class="note-label">Quick check (on paper)</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Given <code>marks = {'{'}75, 80, 93, 68, 88{'}'}</code>, what is <code>marks[3]</code>?</li>
          <li>Insert 27 at index 2 of [11, 22, 33, 44, _]. Which values move, and in what order?</li>
          <li>Delete index 1 of [10, 25, 30, 40, _]. Which values move, and in what order?</li>
          <li>Which of these would a linked list make easier, and why?</li>
        </ol>
        <details class="reveal"><summary>Show the answers</summary><div class="flow">
          <ol>
            <li><b>68</b>. Count from 0: 75, 80, 93, <b>68</b>.</li>
            <li>44 moves to index 4 first, then 33 moves to index 3. Then 27 goes into index 2: [11, 22, 27, 33, 44].</li>
            <li>30 moves to index 1 first, then 40 moves to index 2. Size drops to 3: [10, 30, 40]. (Slot 3 still holds an old 40, but it is not counted.)</li>
            <li>Insertion and deletion (questions 2 and 3). A linked list changes one or two links instead of shifting values, once it is at the spot. Access (question 1) is easier in an array.</li>
          </ol>
        </div></details>
      </div>
      <div class="paper flow">
        <p class="note-label">Coding practice</p>
        <p>Using <code>int[] marks = {'{'}78, 85, 91, 74, 88{'}'};</code>, write a program that:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>prints all five scores using a loop;</li>
          <li>finds and prints the highest score;</li>
          <li>prints only the scores that are 75 and above;</li>
          <li>counts and prints the number of passing scores.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Checklist: Did you use a loop? Did <code>highest</code> start with <code>marks[0]</code>? Did you use a condition for scores <code>&gt;= 75</code>? You can run it in the <a href="https://www.programiz.com/java-programming/online-compiler/">Programiz online compiler</a>.</p>
        <details class="reveal"><summary>Show one possible answer</summary><div class="flow">
          <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(practice)} /></pre>
          <TryIt />
          <p>Output: 78, 85, 91, 74, 88 (one per line), then <code>Highest: 91</code>, then 78, 85, 91, 88, then <code>Passing: 4</code>.</p>
        </div></details>
      </div>
      <div class="paper flow">
        <p class="note-label">Your coding task: remove a value</p>
        <p>This task joins Part 3 and Part 4: find a value, then delete it, like crossing a sold-out item off a canteen list. Finish <code>removeValue</code> so it removes the first copy of <code>target</code> and returns the new size. <code>indexOf</code> is finished for you and finds the index. Write about 5 lines where it says YOUR CODE, then run it: the four printed lines must match the comments.</p>
